## Methodology

### Phase 1 — diagnosis

The W44-200 finding pointed at DCT32x32 Y (bucket 3) Lehmer emitting
308 codes vs Libjxl-mode's 5 / cjxl's ~5. Hypothesised 3 candidates:
(1) gate divergence, (2) upstream coefficient divergence, (3)
`compute_coeff_order` algorithm divergence.

W44-201 per-block dump (`examples/w44_201_coeffs_dump.rs` +
`vardct/w44_76_dump.rs::dump_coeffs`) on 3637739 LOSER + 1418519
WINNER × Zenjxl + Libjxl modes captured every non-zero coefficient
of every DCT32x32 Y first-block. Per-position zero-count dump
(`compute_custom_orders` env-gated dump) captured the per-(bucket,
channel, position) zero count.

**Diagnostic outcome**: on the 34 shared DCT32x32 Y blocks across
Zenjxl-vs-Libjxl modes (where both pick DCT32x32 Y), the coefficient
VALUES are bit-identical (405/405). Candidate 2 (upstream) RULED
OUT for shared blocks. The divergence is in WHICH blocks each mode
picks — Zenjxl picks 61 DCT32x32 Y first-blocks (matches cjxl 98.4%),
Libjxl-mode picks only 42 (60.8% agreement with cjxl). Libjxl-mode's
fewer blocks → flatter per-position zero distribution → 2 effective
quantized bins → minimal sort permutation → 5 Lehmer codes.

For Zenjxl with 61 blocks, the per-position max zero count is 61,
landing the distribution in 3 quantized bins (q=0/1/2) via the
`count * (1/sqrt(1024)) + 0.1` formula. The middle 700 positions
fall into q=2 and get sorted to the end of the scan, producing a
308-Lehmer permutation. The W44-82 cost-benefit model
(`total_savings_bits = (nzeros_custom - nzeros_natural) * max_count`)
overestimates the AC saving at 21492 bits (assuming 1 bit per
trailing zero per block) when the empirical cost is closer to
0.3-0.5 bits per trailing zero — so the gate admits the permutation
when its true savings don't justify the ~330 B Lehmer overhead.

cjxl (with similar 62 DCT32x32 Y first-blocks) presumably hits the
same quantization but encodes a similar Lehmer permutation more
efficiently via ANS context choice or simply pays the cost without
gating. Our gate's mis-calibrated model is the proximate cause of
the Pareto-loser.

### Phase 2 — fix selection

W44-201 evaluated 3 mechanisms:

1. **Variant C (SHIPPED)**: hard-disable buckets 3 + 6 from
   cost-benefit admission. Simple, narrow, byte-identical wherever
   the gate wasn't firing (every cell where bucket 3/6 didn't pass
   cost-benefit pre-W44-201). Wins are concentrated where the
   model was overshooting; zero new regressions.
2. **Variant B**: disable bucket 3 only. -384 B on 3637739 (the
   target cell), but misses additional wins on cells where bucket
   6 also overshoots (e.g. 1531677 d=3 saves -3.19% with B vs
   -6.97% with C).
3. **Savings-factor tightening** (`JXL_W44_201_SAVINGS_FACTOR=0.3`):
   measured -0.33% total bytes — half the win of Variant C — and
   leaves the cost-model architecture untouched. Could be a
   complementary refinement but not a substitute.

Variant C ships. The decision is documented in `gate_registry.rs`
via the `coeff_orders_disable_large_buckets` Section D divergence
field. Default `true` for Zenjxl/Aggressive/LeanFaster; `false`
for Libjxl (preserves libjxl-parity).

